WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.194
Untrusted content enters an ASP.

2
00:00:02.194 --> 00:00:17.274
NET Core application through many paths: form fields, route values, query strings, headers, API requests, file imports, CMS authoring tools, support tickets, logs, databases, message queues, and third-party integrations.

3
00:00:17.274 --> 00:00:20.222
Some values arrive directly from a browser.

4
00:00:20.222 --> 00:00:24.061
Others arrive through a trusted-looking internal system.

5
00:00:24.061 --> 00:00:29.750
Either way, the rendering decision still matters when the value is later displayed.

6
00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:32.629
Stored content deserves special attention.

7
00:00:32.629 --> 00:00:45.997
A product description, profile field, support note, audit message, or imported record may be written today and rendered weeks later in a different page, component, export, or administrative view.

8
00:00:45.997 --> 00:00:48.396
Storage does not make content safe.

9
00:00:48.396 --> 00:00:52.920
It only changes when and where the rendering decision will happen.

10
00:00:52.920 --> 00:01:03.340
Trust boundaries exist between browser clients, server-side views, APIs, databases, administrators, CMS systems, partner systems, and operational tools.

11
00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:06.767
Content can cross those boundaries more than once.

12
00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:18.558
A value may begin in a public form, move into a moderation queue, appear in an administrator dashboard, feed an email template, and later surface in a customer-facing page.

13
00:01:18.558 --> 00:01:22.054
Each rendering point needs an appropriate decision.

14
00:01:22.054 --> 00:01:25.961
Administrative screens and internal tools are not exempt.

15
00:01:25.961 --> 00:01:32.747
They often display raw operational content, imported records, user messages, or diagnostic details.

16
00:01:32.747 --> 00:01:40.699
If an internal tool renders untrusted text as markup, it can put privileged users and high-impact workflows at risk.

17
00:01:40.699 --> 00:01:44.263
Internal does not automatically mean safe rendering.

18
00:01:44.263 --> 00:01:49.953
A practical review technique is to map untrusted data flow from entry to rendering.

19
00:01:49.953 --> 00:02:01.606
Identify where data enters, where it is stored, how it is transformed, where it is displayed, where it is exported, and which reusable helpers or components are involved.

20
00:02:01.606 --> 00:02:08.324
This map helps reviewers find the exact places where output context and encoding decisions happen.

21
00:02:08.324 --> 00:02:11.546
Data flow review also improves maintainability.

22
00:02:11.546 --> 00:02:23.131
When developers know where content changes trust level or display context, they can choose clearer DTOs, safer view models, approved rendering helpers, and better tests.

23
00:02:23.131 --> 00:02:31.220
The result is not only fewer XSS risks, but a codebase that is easier to reason about when rendering behavior changes.
